  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nSurface analysis depicted strong surface ridging directly over \nSW KS. With only some wispy cirrus, radiational cooling was near\noptimal levels this evening, with light and variable winds \nallowing temperatures to fall quickly. All SW KS reporting sites\nwere already near to well below freezing as of 11 pm, including\nmid 20s across the northeast counties. NBM minimum temperature \ngrid is not cold enough for Monday morning, and reduced sunrise \ntemperatures 3-5 degrees across the board, more in line with \n00z MAV. Expecting lower 20s to be common at sunrise, with our \ncoldest locations such as the Arkansas valley in the northwest \nzones in the upper teens. This will be an efficiently long \nfreeze to finally end the growing season for all SW KS zones, \nwith many locations experiencing multiple hours well below \n28 degrees. Winds Monday morning will be light and variable or \nlight southeast, so wind chills will not be a factor. \n\nLarge cool surface anticyclone encompassing much of the plains\nMonday will slowly weaken over SW KS this afternoon, allowing\nsoutheast winds to increase modestly. SE wind gusts of \n25-30 mph are expected Monday afternoon, especially west of \nUS 83, nearest the approaching shortwave. Southeast winds will\nsimply recirculate the continental polar air, and with 850 mb\ntemperatures near -1 to -2C all day (and given a cold start this\nmorning) afternoon temperatures will struggle into the lower\n40s. NBM is on the cool side of guidance today and was accepted. \n\nShortwave near the Four Corners 6 pm Monday will reach the \nSW KS/W TX plains midday Tuesday. Mid and high clouds will\nrapidly increase Monday evening as lift increases. This\nshortwave remains consistently forecast to be weak, progressive\nand moisture-starved, and will come through SW KS with little\nfanfare. A brief period of synoptic ascent north of the\nshortwave remains focused on the 6 am-noon Tuesday timeframe,\nand this weak window of lift will be focused west of US 83 and\nfavoring the Colorado border. Here, a light dusting is possible\nin spots Tuesday morning, and some slick spots are possible on\nelevated roadways such as bridges and overpasses. Some models\nsuggest up to one inch of snow is possible in portions of Morton\ncounty/Elkhart, but most locations will receive much less than\nthat. NBM probability of >1 inch of snow is highest, near 30%,\nacross SE Colorado/Baca County. At any rate, impacts will be \nlittle to none. \n\nSubsidence behind the departing shortwave will clear the sky\nrapidly late Tuesday, but the passing system will reinforce the\ncold air, keeping afternoon temperatures stuck in the upper 30s\nand lower 40s. Another widespread hard freeze is forecast\nWednesday morning, with most locations in the lower 20s. \n\nModels keep cool surface high pressure in control over Kansas\nWednesday and Thursday, which will keep afternoon temperatures\nseasonably cool, mainly in the 40s, with recurring freezes in\nthe 20s at night. Forecast is completely dry Wednesday through\nthe upcoming weekend, with excellent Thanksgiving holiday travel\nconditions expected across the plains.",
